{"_meta":{"com.oxinsider/docs-group":"games","securitySchemes":[{"scopes":["read"],"type":"oauth2"}]},"annotations":{"destructiveHint":false,"idempotentHint":true,"openWorldHint":false,"readOnlyHint":true,"title":"Get one game"},"description":"The same game object list_games returns, for one event_slug: both sides with their provider ids and live scores, the UTC kickoff, the provider's own status, the esports series format, a tennis game's tournament edition (tier, place, venue time zone, draw and dates), and the game's winner markets (the moneyline, or a soccer game's three 1X2 legs) with their condition_id, outcome token ids and provider moneyline prices. For the game's spreads, totals and player props, call list_game_markets with the same event_slug. The slug is the identity the live_sports_updated webhook pulse carries, so a receiver can read the full game straight from a pulse.\n\nArgs:\n  - event_slug (string, required): the game's event slug, for example nfl-buf-nyj-2026-09-22. Matched case-insensitively\n\nReturns: the game object with the envelope's meta. status.state keeps postponed, cancelled, suspended and delayed distinct from a plain 'not live'. Each game's own coverage reports whether scores, competitor provider ids and the schedule were available: when competitors reads labels, do not join on names. A slug outside the published coverage answers 404, including a real Polymarket event in a sport this deployment does not serve; list_games names what is covered. Each winner market carries settlement, the block list_game_markets serves (status observed, not_posed or unavailable; oracle_status, payouts, result outcome or split, the 50-50; resolved_at); it never waits for Polymarket: a market we saw settle reads its recorded settlement, and any other market of a game not read lately reads unavailable, and a read a moment later may answer. Carries no sharp-money split and no holder identity.","inputSchema":{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"event_s